concepts.body:
Every request passes through a **pipeline** of middleware components, like a letter passing several desks in an office. Each one can look at the request, call the next component, and act on the response on its way back. Or it can end the request early by writing a response itself, without calling the next one.

```csharp
app.Use(async (context, next) =>
{
    // before
    await next(context);
    // after: the response has been produced
});
```

`app.Run(...)` adds a final middleware that never calls a next one, and endpoints (`MapGet` etc.) run at the end of the pipeline.

**Order matters**, because middleware runs in the order you add it. A typical order:

1. `UseExceptionHandler` — turns crashes into error responses (first, so it catches everything after it)
2. `UseHsts` / `UseHttpsRedirection` — send callers to the secure `https://` version of your site
3. `UseStaticFiles` — serves files such as images as they are (can answer directly)
4. `UseRouting` — picks the endpoint (added for you if you don't)
5. `UseCors` — controls which other websites' pages may call your API
6. `UseAuthentication` (works out who the caller is), then `UseAuthorization` (checks they're allowed)
7. Endpoints

Working out who the caller is is called **authentication**; checking what they may do is **authorization**. Putting `UseAuthorization` before `UseAuthentication`, or `UseCors` after them, are classic mistakes.

Once the response has started (headers sent), you can't change the status code or headers; check `context.Response.HasStarted`. For reusable middleware, write a class with an `InvokeAsync(HttpContext)` method and register it with `app.UseMiddleware<T>()`. ASP.NET Core creates such a class once for the whole app — a **singleton** — so take scoped services as `InvokeAsync` parameters rather than constructor parameters.

